            {
              "id": "glog-6fda3384-9646-4a28-b0c6-87c6febfcd3e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `snprintf`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function like `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(temp, name.c_str(), sizeof(temp));\ntemp[sizeof(temp) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `name.c_str()` to `temp`, but it will not copy more than `sizeof(temp)` characters. The last line ensures that `temp` is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions.\n- `<string>`: This library provides the `std::string` class.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
