              "id": "glog-a8f3d8ce-a879-4a98-beca-594f81e2d48d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. This can lead to unexpected behavior, including program crashes, data corruption, and execution of arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `memcpy` function is used to copy `serialized_code_object_size` bytes from `serialized_code_object` to `code_object_alloc_data`. If `serialized_code_object_size` is larger than the size of `code_object_alloc_data`, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t code_object_alloc_data_size = sizeof(code_object_alloc_data);\nif (serialized_code_object_size <= code_object_alloc_data_size) {\n    memcpy(code_object_alloc_data, serialized_code_object, serialized_code_object_size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: serialized_code_object is too large to fit in code_object_alloc_data\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first get the size of `code_object_alloc_data` using the `sizeof` operator. We then check if `serialized_code_object_size` is less than or equal to `code_object_alloc_data_size` before calling `memcpy`. If `serialized_code_object_size` is larger, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
